A||N||4||contra||How can i fix my poor brakes?||||1||
Z||000000||contra||08-13-2000||05:58 PM||||i've got a slight problem i'm ''assuming'' with my fiero's brakes. The pads were checked by my mechanic and so were the rotors. I even had one ground down a bit because supposedly it had some rust on it or something. But anyways, i still get a grinding sound out of my rear driver side brake, my front driver side brake squeels a little (but i think it's just cause of metallic brake pads), and my pedal feels like it's almost going to the floor. I have to press really really hard if i want to stop quickly, otherwise i just sort of stop slowly. Is that normal, or should i try bleeding my brake lines in case of bubbles? Or could it be a caliper? I've got a complete fiero braking system as a spare if there is something majorly wrong, but i just want some opinions from you people before i start to think the worst  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||08-13-2000||07:44 PM||||I don't think that they're supposed to but both the 84 I had and the 86 I have now do the same thing as far as stopping.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rrazzz||08-13-2000||07:51 PM||||A rusted disk usually means the caliper is not working (pads are not rubbing on the disk). That would also expain the poor stopping.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-13-2000||07:53 PM||||Metallic brake pads last forever but they don't stop very well and they ruin rotors. You should replace your metallic pads with good quality semi-metallic brake pads. The carbon metallic brake pads are good in warn climates but if you live in a clod climate they tend to suck when they are cold.<P>Your rotors when turned should have a non-directional surface usually applied by a sanding disc with a air grinder by the mechanic while the rotor is still on the lathe. If you get your rotors turned and they have that thread pattern left by the brake lathe on them than they will take forever to break in and won't stop very well until they do. The threads could also cause the pads to thrust to one side in the direction of the threads and you get a clunking noise every time you hit your brakes. This is usually only a problem with drum brakes though. <P>If you have warped rotors or excessive parallelism those conditions can cause pad knock back where the warped rotors hit the pads and knock them back away from the rotors. This in turn causes excessive brake pedal travel because when you apply the brakes the caliper has to take up that clearance caused by pad knock back. Disc brake pads are supposed to have 0 clearance between the rotor and the pad.<P>You should also completely flush the braking system by bleeding every wheel until clear brake fluid comes out. Check your calipers for binding sliders by looking at the pads for uneven wear. Sometimes you can just purchase caliper grease or silicone grease and grease the slides after you have cleaned them. <P>After you have gone on a long drive get out of your car and feel each wheel for heat. They should all have the same amount of heat. If one wheel is significantly hotter than the other wheel than the hotter wheel indicates you have a frozen or binding caliper, replace the caliper if you do.<P>Hope this information helps you.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||contra||08-13-2000||08:39 PM||||thanx, i'll try all of the above.<BR>i am fairly sure i'm using semi metallic pads, i didn't know they had metallic AND semi metallic, so my bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>but anyhow, i'll try the bleeding process when it comes back from being painted. Also, you could be right that because the rotor was recently ground, it could take a while to break in.<BR>thanx again<BR>||||reg||1
